This file adds architecture, error-handling, and security rules on top of the persona and TypeScript/Code Style rules in `project-details.mdc`. Every rule includes a WHY explanation for the reasoning behind it.
For naming, TypeScript typing, and general code style, follow `project-details.mdc`. For function-size/complexity, follow `clean-function-and-method.mdc`. Do not restate those here.

## Architecture
- Clean Architecture with dependency inversion. Domain layer is framework-agnostic.
  > WHY: Testable business logic that survives framework changes.
- Repository pattern for data access. Never call ORM directly from business logic.
  > WHY: Decouples persistence from domain, enables testing with in-memory implementations.
- For Prisma/ORM call isolation (dedicated method, try/catch), follow `backend.mdc`.
  > WHY: Keeps this rule single-sourced next to the rest of the Prisma conventions.

## Error Handling
- At HTTP and GraphQL boundaries, throw NestJS `HttpException` subclasses such as `BadRequestException`, `NotFoundException`, and `ForbiddenException`. For internal failures, use dedicated `Error` subclasses and map them at the boundary.
  > WHY: This matches the current NestJS codebase and keeps domain/internal errors decoupled from transport concerns.
- Use the project logger (`@feature-corner/nestjs-logger` or the local Nest logger wrapper). Never log sensitive data (passwords, tokens, PII).
  > WHY: Observability without security risk. Structured logs enable alerting.

## Testing
- For coverage targets and mocking rules, follow `testing.mdc`.
  > WHY: Testing conventions have a single source of truth, scoped to test files.

## Security
- For input validation at API boundaries (DTOs, `ValidationPipe`, GraphQL input types), follow `backend.mdc`.
  > WHY: Keeps validation guidance single-sourced next to the rest of the Nest.js conventions.
- Rate limiting, security headers, and TLS are handled at the infrastructure/gateway layer in this repository. Do not add helmet or throttler packages unless a task explicitly requires application-level enforcement.
  > WHY: Keeps application code aligned with the deployed architecture and avoids duplicating gateway responsibilities.

## Git
- Follow `.cursor/rules/git.mdc` for commit, PR, and secret-handling conventions.
  > WHY: Git workflow guidance has a single source of truth.
